---
import SocialShare from '~/components/common/SocialShare.astro';
import V9Layout from '~/layouts/V9Layout.astro';
import Content from '~/components/v9/content/Content.astro';
import { publicLinks } from '~/data/v9/preview-links';
import { fetchPosts } from '~/utils/blog';
import { getPermalink } from '~/utils/permalinks';
const post = (await fetchPosts()).find(
  (post) => post.slug === 'blog/designing-for-trust-privacy-transparency-respect'
)!;

const shareUrl = new URL(Astro.url.pathname, 'https://www.tribera.ai').href;
---

<V9Layout
  title="Designing for trust — tribera"
  description="Most people looking for a job already have one. Why we made privacy the default rather than a setting — and what happened when we did."
  headerLinkOverrides={publicLinks}
  footerLinkOverrides={publicLinks}
  openGraphType="article"
  image="~/assets/images/blog/trust-by-design.png"
>
  <Content kind="article">
    <div class="reading" aria-hidden="true"><span class="reading__f"></span></div>

    <header class="ahead band">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="ahead__meta">
          <time datetime={post.publishDate.toISOString()}
            >{
              post.publishDate.toLocaleDateString('en-GB', {
                day: 'numeric',
                month: 'short',
                year: 'numeric',
                timeZone: 'UTC',
              })
            }</time
          ><i>·</i>
          {post.category && <a href={getPermalink(post.category.slug, 'category')}>{post.category.title}</a>}<i>·</i
          ><span>{post.readingTime} read</span>
        </p>
        <h1>Designing for trust</h1>
        <p class="ahead__sub">
          Most people looking for a job already have one. Everything we built follows from that, including the thing we
          got wrong for a year.
        </p>
        <div class="ahead__by">
          <span class="ahead__ph" id="byPh" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="ahead__who"><b>Abhishek Prasad</b>Co-founder &amp; CBO · built the signal engine</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="band band--w">
      <div class="wrap">
        <article class="art">
          <p class="lede">
            The person you most want to hire has a job, is reasonably happy in it, and has not opened their CV in three
            years. Almost everything we built follows from that one fact.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a version of hiring that assumes the market is full of people looking. Post the role, collect the
            applications, filter down. It works for volume roles and it works when the economy is bad, and the rest of
            the time it quietly selects for the people with the most availability rather than the most judgment.
          </p>

          <p>
            The engineer you actually want has been somewhere four years, is in the middle of something she cares about,
            and will look at exactly one opportunity this year. She will look at it quietly. And she will only look at
            all if looking costs her nothing.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the design question was never how to attract more candidates. It was <b
              >how to make a conversation safe enough to be worth having.</b
            >
          </p>

          <h2>What quietly has to mean</h2>

          <p>
            Not searchable. Your profile does not appear in a directory, a feed, or a recruiter’s saved search. There is
            no version of this where someone stumbles across you.
          </p>

          <p>
            Blockable by name. You list your current employer, and anyone else you would rather not meet, and those
            companies never see that you exist. Not a filtered result — no result.
          </p>

          <p>
            No direct contact. Companies cannot message you. Every request comes through us, which means you find out
            that someone is interested without them finding out you were looking.
          </p>

