              ></rect><rect x="66" y="92" width="15" height="11" rx="2.5" fill="#DA0007" opacity="1"></rect><rect
                x="86"
                y="92"
                width="15"
                height="11"
                rx="2.5"
                fill="#C6C2B8"
                opacity="0.28"></rect><rect x="106" y="92" width="15" height="11" rx="2.5" fill="#C6C2B8" opacity="0.28"
              ></rect><rect x="126" y="92" width="15" height="11" rx="2.5" fill="#C6C2B8" opacity="0.28"></rect><rect
                x="146"
                y="92"
                width="15"
                height="11"
                rx="2.5"
                fill="#C6C2B8"
                opacity="0.28"></rect><rect x="166" y="92" width="15" height="11" rx="2.5" fill="#C6C2B8" opacity="0.28"
              ></rect><rect x="186" y="92" width="15" height="11" rx="2.5" fill="#C6C2B8" opacity="0.28"></rect></svg
            >
            <figcaption>
              <b>Visibility only ever moves one way, and only when you move it.</b> Before you do anything, nobody. After
              the interview, us. Then one named team, then that team’s panel — each step released by you, and reversible.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <h2>The thing we had backwards</h2>

          <p>
            We built the privacy controls first and made them opt-in. Sensible-looking: give people the switches, let
            them decide. A settings page with four toggles, all off by default, clearly explained.
          </p>

          <p>
            Almost nobody turned them on. Not because they did not care — because they never got that far. Someone
            weighing up whether it is safe to explore does not sign up and then go looking for the safety features. They
            decide on the landing page, in about eleven seconds, and if the answer is not obviously yes they close the
            tab.
          </p>

          <blockquote class="pull">
            A privacy setting is a promise you make later.
            <em>A default is a promise you have already kept.</em>
          </blockquote>

          <figure class="fig">
            <svg class="fig__svg" viewBox="0 0 240 58" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-hidden="true"
              ><rect x="0" y="6" width="44" height="14" rx="7" fill="#C6C2B8" opacity="0.45"></rect><rect
                x="0"
                y="34"
                width="232"
                height="14"
                rx="7"
                fill="#DA0007"
                opacity="1"></rect></svg
            >
            <figcaption>
              Opt-in privacy, then default privacy. <b
                >The controls did not change. Only which way they pointed when you arrived.</b
              >
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <p>
            So we inverted it. Hidden is the default state, not the protected one. You are invisible from the moment you
            arrive, and everything after that is you deciding to be slightly less invisible to one named company at a
            time.
          </p>

          <h2>Privacy without transparency is just a black box</h2>

          <p>
            The other half of trust is harder, and most platforms skip it: if we are going to score you, you have to be
            able to see the score.
          </p>

          <p>
            You see the same breakdown the hiring team sees. The same four dimensions, the same numbers, including the
            one you came out weakest on and what specifically would move it. There is no separate, gentler version
            prepared for the candidate — partly because that would be dishonest, and partly because the moment two
            versions exist, someone eventually shows you the wrong one.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is uncomfortable to build. It is the only version that survives a candidate asking why they were ranked
            third.
          </p>

          <h2>We check the company too</h2>

          <p>
            Every job board asks a candidate to prove themselves to a company that has proved nothing to anyone. Before
            a request reaches you, the team on the other end has to clear its own checks: is the headcount actually
            funded, how many rounds, how fast do they decide, how long has the manager been in post, and did the last
            few people they hired stay.
          </p>

          <p>
            When something does not clear, you still see the request — with the flag attached and the reason written
            out. You decide whether it matters. We are not in a position to decide that for you, and pretending
            otherwise would be its own kind of disrespect.
          </p>

          <h2>Why any of this is worth the engineering</h2>

          <p>
            Because the cost of getting it wrong is not ours. A candidate whose employer finds out they were looking
            does not lose a feature. They lose standing, a project, sometimes a job — and they lose it because of a
            system they trusted for about a week.
          </p>

          <p>
            That asymmetry is the whole argument. We carry a product risk. They carry a career risk. The defaults should
            reflect who has more to lose, and almost none of them do.
          </p>

          <p class="signoff">Belong. Build. Become.</p>
        </article>
        <div class="aft">
          <div class="atags">
            {
              post.tags?.map((tag) => (
                <a class="atag" href={getPermalink(tag.slug, 'tag')}>
                  {tag.title}
                </a>
              ))
            }
          </div>
          <SocialShare class="ashare" url={shareUrl} text={post.title} />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap band" id="related">
      <div class="shead" style="grid-template-columns:1fr;text-align:center;justify-items:center;gap:8px">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--red" data-v9-reveal>Keep reading</p>
          <h2 class="h2" data-v9-reveal style="--d:60ms;max-width:none;margin-bottom:clamp(16px,2vw,24px)">
            Three more that came out of real mandates.
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p class="dek" data-v9-reveal style="--d:100ms;max-width:none">
          Or go back to <a
            href="/blog"
            style="color:var(--red);
    text-decoration:none;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(218,0,7,.35)"
            >everything we’ve published</a
          >.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rel" data-v9-reveal style="--d:140ms">
        <a class="relc" href="/blog/rethinking-hiring-metrics-signal-efficiency"
          ><p class="relc__k">Hiring strategy</p><p class="relc__t">Rethinking hiring metrics</p><p class="relc__x">
            Time-to-hire measures your speed. Interview-to-hire measures your aim.
          </p></a
        ><a class="relc" href="/blog/when-job-hunting-feels-like-a-black-hole"
          ><p class="relc__k">Candidate experience</p><p class="relc__t">When job hunting feels like a black hole</p><p
            class="relc__x"
          >
            Silence is not rejection, but it costs the same.
          </p></a
        ><a class="relc" href="/blog/designing-ai-for-high-stakes-technical-assessment"
          ><p class="relc__k">AI and assessment</p><p class="relc__t">Designing AI for high-stakes assessment</p><p
            class="relc__x"
          >
            A model can read a transcript. It cannot tell you whether the person meant it.
          </p></a
        >
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="trust band" id="cta">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="sub" style="text-align:center;display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center">
          <p class="sub__k">If you’re the one looking</p>
          <p class="sub__h">Hidden from the first second.</p>
          <p class="sub__s">
            Half an hour with someone who has done your job, scored against the same rubric the hiring team sees.
          </p>
          <div class="btns" style="justify-content:center">
            <a href="/candidates" class="btn btn--light">Create your profile</a>
            <a href="/blog" class="btn btn--wire">Read the rest</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band band--w close" id="contact">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="shead" style="grid-template-columns:1fr;text-align:center;justify-items:center;gap:8px">
          <div>
            <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--red" data-v9-reveal>Two doors</p>
            <h2 class="h2" data-v9-reveal style="max-width:none">Two doors. Same room.</h2>
          </div>
          <p class="dek" data-v9-reveal style="--d:60ms;max-width:none">
            If you have a role you’re stuck on, or you’re the one being interviewed, <b
              >both of those start the same way.</b
            >
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="doors" data-v9-reveal style="--d:110ms">
          <div class="door">
            <p class="door__k">If you’re hiring</p>
            <p class="door__t">Three names you’d take any of.</p>
            <p class="door__d">
              A hundred signals, a senior expert on each shortlisted person, and the reasoning behind every rank.
            </p>
            <p class="door__next"><span>Next</span> A 45-minute intake call. Three names back inside 36 hours.</p>
            <a href="/" class="btn btn--dark">See how one role ran</a>
          </div>
          <div class="door">
            <p class="door__k">If you’re looking</p>
            <p class="door__t">One conversation, then teams come to you.</p>
            <p class="door__d">
              Half an hour with someone who has done your job. Anonymous until you decide otherwise.
            </p>
            <p class="door__next"><span>Next</span> Fifteen minutes to set up, and you keep the score.</p>
            <a href="/candidates" class="btn btn--ghost">Create your profile</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="bbb2" data-v9-reveal><span>Belong</span><i></i><span>Build</span><i></i><span>Become</span></p>
        <p class="bbb2__s" data-v9-reveal>Tribe + Era. A hire only works if both sides were right about each other.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <script>
      /* ══ reading progress ══ */
      (function () {
        const bar = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.reading__f'),
          art = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.art');
        if (!bar || !art) return;
        function tick() {
          if (!art || !bar) return;
          const r = art.getBoundingClientRect(),
            vh = innerHeight;
          const total = r.height - vh * 0.4;
          const done = Math.min(Math.max(-r.top + vh * 0.4, 0), Math.max(total, 1));
          bar.style.width = (done / Math.max(total, 1)) * 100 + '%';
        }
        addEventListener('scroll', tick, { passive: true });
        addEventListener('resize', tick);
        tick();
      })();
    </script>
  </Content>
</V9Layout>
